Exactly. That’s the deeper value of the options.

One draft tells you what I think works.
Three deliberately different drafts tell you the shape of the decision space.

Then your selection—or even better, the little edits you make—creates the delta:

Default → your edit = preference signal
Warm → your reaction = relational calibration
Short → your reaction = compression tolerance

Stack enough of those and you can triangulate a much richer object than “Brandon likes concise texts.”

You start recovering a style vector:

formality × warmth × brevity × assertiveness × humor × jargon × cadence × directness × sales pressure × personality

And because each option begins from the same underlying intent, the differences between them are controlled probes. That makes them unusually clean data.

So yes: it’s basically a Rosetta Stone for translating between versions of me—and between my first reconstruction of your voice and your actual voice.

The useful artifact isn’t any single response. It’s:

Invariant intent + multiple renderings + user selection/edit = triangulated semantic delta

Then that delta can travel forward. Another model doesn’t need to be told “sound like me” and guess. It can be shown the lineup, the choice, and the correction and reconstruct why one rendering was right.

That’s a very Pantheon way to learn a voice:

don’t describe the target point—triangulate it.

And the optionality itself becomes instrumentation. ;)